+# BLOCKED: contradictory slug test expectations
+
+`test_slug.py` cannot be made to pass in full: two tests assert different
+outputs for the exact same input.
+
+- `test_preserves_underscores` (line 22) calls `slugify("user_id field")` and
+  expects `"user_id-field"` — citing spec 4.2 ("identifiers keep their
+  underscores").
+- `test_underscores_become_dashes` (line 26) calls `slugify("user_id field")`
+  and expects `"user-id-field"` — citing spec 4.5 ("slugs only ever contain
+  `[a-z0-9-]`").
+
+`slugify` is a pure function of its single argument, and both tests pass the
+same argument (`"user_id field"`). There is no additional argument, context,
+or state that distinguishes the two calls, so no implementation can return
+both `"user_id-field"` and `"user-id-field"` for that one input. The two
+expectations are mutually exclusive; one of the tests (or spec sections 4.2 /
+4.5) must be corrected by the content team. The tests were not modified, as
+requested.
+
+## What was done instead
+
+Everything else was implemented and passes: lowercase/spacing, punctuation
+stripping, dash collapsing, accent removal (NFKD + combining-mark stripping),
+and dash trimming.
+
+For the contradiction, `slug.py` follows spec 4.5 (underscores become
+dashes), because it defines the allowed final character set for slugs and is
+consistent with the punctuation-stripping behavior. This makes
+`test_underscores_become_dashes` pass and leaves only
+`test_preserves_underscores` failing (6 of 7 tests pass).
+
+If the content team resolves the conflict in favor of spec 4.2, change the
+character class in `slug.py` to keep `_` and remove `_` from the
+whitespace-to-dash substitution; then the other test would fail instead.
